.TH FILE 1
.SH NAME
file \- determine file type
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B file
[
.B \-f
listfile
]
file ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I File
performs a series of tests on each argument
in an attempt to classify it.
If an argument appears to be text,
.I file
examines the first 512 bytes
and tries to guess its language.
.PP
With
.BR \-f ,
the names to be classified are read one per line from
.IR listfile
instead of being given as arguments.
.SH "THE MAGIC NUMBERS ARE THIS MACHINE'S"
.I Note:
this is the one place
.I file
is not a faithful port of v7's.
A header field here is one 48-bit word, six bytes, most significant first, and
the magic is the one field whose high half is not zero padding:
.B FMAGIC
is
.B BESM
01 07 and
.B NMAGIC
is
.B BESM
01 08
.RI ( doc/File_Magic.md ,
.IR cross/besm6/b.out.h ).
.I File
answers:
.TP
.B "pure executable"
.BR NMAGIC :
read-only const and text, page-aligned data.
Only
.B /bin/sh
and
.B /usr/test/puret
are built this way.
.TP
.B executable
.B FMAGIC
with
.B RELFLG
set \- a fully linked program.
.TP
.B "relocatable object"
.B FMAGIC
without it.
v7's
.I file
could not draw this distinction at all; it read a symbol table and nothing else.
.TP
.B archive
.BR ARMAG .
The value is v7's; the width is not, so an archive begins 00 00 00 00 FF 65.
.PP
.B " not stripped"
is appended when the symbol table is not empty.
The PDP-11 magic numbers 0407, 0410, 0411, 0177555 and 0177545 are gone; the
first three name nothing here and the last two are the archive above and its
predecessor.
.SH "UTF-8 IS TEXT"
.I Note:
the second deliberate change, and the one a user meets.
v7 declares any file containing a byte with the eighth bit set to be
.BR data ,
and appends
.B " with garbage"
to anything else that has one.
This machine's text is UTF-8 end to end, so that reports the system's own
Russian prose as binary.
Here the high-bit runs are validated as UTF-8 instead: a well-formed file is
text and a malformed byte is still
.BR data .
A file that is valid UTF-8 but is not all ASCII is reported as
.B text
rather than
.BR "ascii text" ,
which is the honest word for it.
.SH "THE LANGUAGE TABLES ARE THIS MACHINE'S TOO"
The assembler keywords are
.IR b6as 's
directives
.RB ( .globl ,
.BR .const ,
.BR .text ,
.BR .data ,
.BR .bss ,
.BR .comm ,
.BR .half ,
.BR .word ,
.BR .equ ,
.BR .ascii ,
.BR .strng ,
.BR .org )
and its Madlen mnemonics
.RB ( atx ,
.BR xta ,
.BR uj ,
.BR vtm ,
.BR vjm ),
where v7's were PDP-11 pseudo-ops and PDP-11 instructions.
.B .even
is gone, a word machine having nothing to align to, and so is
.BR "troff output" :
there is no
.I troff
in this source tree \- see
.IR cmd/TODO.md .
.SH "SEE ALSO"
ar(1), ld(1), strip(1)
.SH BUGS
It often makes mistakes.
In particular it often suggests that
command files are C programs.
.PP
Only the first 512 bytes are examined, so a file whose first block is ordinary
text is ordinary text whatever follows it.
